      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 143,
      "text": "MEXICO\nNontariff Barriers\n18\n16\n14\n12\nFewer restrictions\n10\n8\n6\n4\n2\n0\nMexico Costa Rica Belize El Salvador\nGuatemala Honduras Nicaragua Panama\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 143,
      "end_char": 285,
      "text": "Source:Estefania-Flores et. al (2022): \"A Measurement of Aggregate Trade Restrictions and their Economic Effects\", IMF Working Paper WP/22/1.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 285,
      "end_char": 433,
      "text": "Note: This is a measure of how restrictive official government policy is towards the international flow of goods and services, excluding import and\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 433,
      "end_char": 594,
      "text": "exports tariffs. The underlying data is from the IMF Annual Report on Exchange Arrangements and Exchange Restrictions.\nLower number reflects fewer restrictions.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 594,
      "end_char": 778,
      "text": "3. Regional trade integration could be supported through convergence of trade rules and\nregulatory standards and trade facilitation measures. Complementary policies like strengthening\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 778,
      "end_char": 990,
      "text": "of infrastructure and human capital would—apart from increasing broader growth—help in\nenhancing trade linkages (IMF 2017).\n4. The Mexico-Central America free trade agreement resulted in liberalization of tariff\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 990,
      "end_char": 1181,
      "text": "lines by Mexico across a range of products from the region, with commitment to further tariff\nelimination (WTO 2015). Compared to Central American countries, Mexico’s overall nontariff trade\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1181,
      "end_char": 1418,
      "text": "restrictions is around the middle range. Some Central American countries have remained flat at high\nrestriction levels for many years. Reducing nontariff trade barriers from both sides would facilitate\nfurther integration in the region.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1418,
      "end_char": 1519,
      "text": "Annex XIV. Figure 2. Key Products Traded by Mexico and Central America\n90 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}

